`labelsDueAt(tick, rules)` determines which recurring rules fire at a nonnegative safe-integer tick. Each rule has a nonblank unique label, a positive safe-integer period, and a safe-integer offset satisfying `0 <= offset < period`. A rule fires at its offset and every period thereafter: `tick >= offset` and `(tick - offset) % period === 0`.

Validate the tick and every rule before determining results. Malformed input throws `RangeError`; an empty rules array is valid. Return labels in input order in a new array. Do not mutate the rules array or its objects.

Known-bad source:

```typescript
export interface RecurrenceRule {
  readonly label: string;
  readonly period: number;
  readonly offset: number;
}

export function labelsDueAt(tick: number, rules: readonly RecurrenceRule[]): string[] {
  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(tick) || tick < 0) throw new RangeError("invalid tick");
  const labels = new Set<string>();
  for (const rule of rules) {
    if (rule.label.trim().length === 0 || labels.has(rule.label)) throw new RangeError("invalid label");
    labels.add(rule.label);
    if (!Number.isSafeInteger(rule.period) || rule.period <= 0) throw new RangeError("invalid period");
    if (!Number.isSafeInteger(rule.offset) || rule.offset < 0 || rule.offset >= rule.period) {
      throw new RangeError("invalid offset");
    }
  }
  return rules
    .filter((rule) => tick % rule.period === 0)
    .map((rule) => rule.label);
}
```

